'''La Baba''' ([[Baliscano language|Baliscano]] meaning 'shelter' or 'abode'; {{Langx|kp|Yvytyra-Kuarahy-Owa|4=hill of the rising sun}}) is an island that sits at the entrance to [[Salto Bay]] within the borders of the [[Castejón|Castejón Metropolis]]. La Baba was once the busiest [[Immigration to Balisca|immigrant]] [[La Baba Immigration Center|inspection and processing station]] in Balisca. From 1892 to 1980, nearly 6 million immigrants arriving at the Alpujarra were processed there; approximately 30% of metropolitan residents may be descended from these immigrants. The southeastern side of the island is a national museum of immigration.

The island is accessible by numerous modes of transport, including [[23 Marzo Bridge|a bridge]], [[La Baba Tramway|an aerial tram]], and the city's [[Castejón Metro|metro]] and [[Alpujarra Ferry|ferry]] systems. Many administrative and public services, such as [[W:Emergency services|emergency services]], are provided from both [[Limón]] and [[Trinacria]], but the island also has its own postal service and pneumatic garbage-disposal system. 

According to the [[2025 Baliscan census]], La Baba has a land area of {{convert|12.66|km2}} with a total population of 42,789.

